Presidential elections were held in [[Haiti]] on 26 November 2000.<ref>[[Dieter Nohlen]] (2005) ''Elections in the Americas: A data handbook, Volume I'', p381 {{ISBN|978-0-19-928357-6}}</ref> The opposition parties, organised into the recently created [[Convergence Démocratique]], boycotted the election after disputing the results of the [[2000 Haitian parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]]. The result was a landslide victory for [[Jean-Bertrand Aristide]], who received 91.7% of the vote with a turnout of around 50%.<ref>[http://newleftreview.org/A2507 Option Zero in Haiti], New Left Review May–June 2004</ref>

Concerns were raised when the [[Organization of American States|Organisation of American States]] conducted a fact-finding mission on the election and found that 10 senatorial seats in the simultaneous [[2000 Haitian parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]] should have gone to a second-round runoff because the candidates did not win an absolute majority as required by the [[Constitution of Haiti|constitution]]. This resulted in the [[European Union]] and the [[United States]] banning economic assistance to the country until 2005, which were supported by Haitian opposition members.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Hufbauer |first1=Gary Clyde |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=g-uzlJDD7DwC |title=Economic Sanctions Reconsidered |last2=Schott |first2=Jeffrey J. |last3=Elliott |first3=Kimberly |publisher=Peterson Institute for International Economics |year=2007 |location=Washington |pages=34 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=CNN.com - Haiti opposition supports threat of U.S. sanctions - September 6, 2000 |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2000/WORLD/americas/09/06/haiti.sanctions.reut/index.html |access-date=2022-09-01 |website=CNN}}</ref>

